     * sinks that can only accept a string: jsPDF's doc.text() and XLSX cell
     * values. Wraps penumbraCloneForExport, so web-only content is dropped and
     * noweb() content is kept, exactly as in the HTML sinks.
     *
     * The point of this over a bare `.text()` is STRUCTURAL NEWLINES.
     * `.text()` is textContent, which never breaks a line: a `<br>` contributes
     * nothing at all and adjacent `<div>`s run together, so the only line
     * breaks a PDF cover ever got were the literal newlines that happened to be
     * in the snippet SOURCE. That made the output depend on how the author had
     * formatted their HTML rather than on what the HTML says. `noweb()` exists
     * to put multi-line blocks on cover pages, so it made the problem routine.
     *
     * The rule is deliberately the SAME one the server-side PDF plugin uses
     * (_penumbra_pdf_plain_text in plugins/pdf_generator.php): <br> and the
     * closing tag of a block element become newlines, everything else is
     * flattened, then each line is whitespace-collapsed and trimmed and blank
     * lines are dropped. Browser-download PDFs and scheduled-email PDFs
     * therefore break their lines in the same places.
     */
    function penumbraExportPlainText($el) {
        const $c = penumbraCloneForExport($el);
        // Charts ride as images in every caller, and a <canvas> has no text
        // content anyway - removing it just keeps the callers identical.
        // <style>/<script> bodies ARE text to textContent, so without this a
        // snippet carrying a style block would dump raw CSS onto the page.
        $c.find('canvas, style, script').remove();
        // OPENING block tags break a line as well as closing ones: with closers
        // alone, `Total: <div>x</div>` came out as one line because the only
        // break landed AFTER x, where the blank-line filter then removed it.
        // The `\b` is what keeps the opening pattern off `</div>`.
        // HARD vs SOFT line breaks.
        //
        // A <br> is something the author TYPED, so `x<br><br>y` means "leave me a
        // blank line". A break implied by a block-tag boundary is structural, and
        // a `</div><br>` pairing emits two of them where the author wanted one.
        // Collapsing blank lines away (what this used to do, inherited from the
        // old by-line rule) fixes the artifact by destroying the intent.
        //
        // So <br> becomes a HARD marker and block boundaries stay soft newlines:
        // blank SOFT lines are dropped, blank HARD lines are kept. `</div><br>`
        // still yields one break; `<br><br>` still yields a blank line.
        // \x1F (unit separator) survives the re-parse below and cannot occur in
        // report text; any stray is stripped at the end regardless.
        const HARD = '\x1F';
        const BLOCK = 'p|div|li|tr|h[1-6]|blockquote|section|article|header|footer|figcaption';
        const html = ($c.html() || '')
            .replace(/<br\s*\/?>/gi, HARD)
            .replace(new RegExp('<(?:' + BLOCK + ')\\b[^>]*>', 'gi'), '\n')
            .replace(new RegExp('</(?:' + BLOCK + ')\\s*>', 'gi'), '\n')
            // EVERY remaining tag becomes a SPACE, not nothing. A label/value
            // pair is written as adjacent spans with no whitespace between the
            // tags - the gap between them is CSS (flex), which textContent
            // cannot see - so stripping to nothing jammed them into
            // "External Funding$10". A space is the smallest thing that keeps
            // them separate words; the per-line collapse below means an
            // already-spaced `</b> InfoEd` does not become a double space.
            .replace(/<[^>]*>/g, ' ');
